  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 525 AM EDT Mon Aug 24 2026\n\nQuiet weather-wise today into Tuesday night/Wed AM, with partly to \nmostly sunny skies, light winds, and highs in the 70s, low 80s. \nOvernight lows will be in the 50s. \n\nNorthwest flow will keep waves on Lake MI in the 2-3 ft range for \nbeaches along Berrien County, MI shoreline today. The higher waves \nwill be further north as winds strengthen slightly there this \nafternoon. Conditions are still moderate swim risk this morning, \ncoming down from high risk last night. Waves are around 2 feet with \n5-6 second periods. \n\nThe surface high pressure/upper level ridge in place today and Tue \nwill slowly drift eastward and break down through Wednesday, with an \nupper level trough crossing the Great Lakes later in the day \nWednesday into Thursday night. This will bring a surface cold front \nthrough our entire forecast area by Thursday morning. Have chances \nfor showers/storms increasing Wed into Thu AM from west to east, \npeaking around 30-60 percent for now (best chances west late Wed \nAft/Eve). SPC added about 2/3rds of our CWA into a marginal risk for \nstrong to severe storms...generally northwest of a line from Marion, \nIN up into Defiance, OH and onto Wauseon, OH. \n\nConfidence is low at this time, especially further east mainly due \nto later arrival time-approx. 5-8 pm EDT Wed in the far west, then \nmoving eastward as we lose daytime heating/instability. Mid level \nlapse rates aren't all that impressive either (bordering on 6 C/km). \nStill, some of the guidance has mixed layer cape in the 1500-2500 \nJ/Kg range further west in that earlier period, with about 25-35kts \nof 0-6 km shear. Dewpoints also reach into the mid-upper 60s, low \n70s. As they mentioned in their discussion, sufficient for \nmulticells and even a few supercells. Main threats are isolated \nlarge hail and damaging wind gusts, but with low LCL's and a bit of \nlow level wind shear (depends on the model), we could see tornado \npotential. At any rate, a lot could change in the next couple of \ndays so will continue to monitor and refine the area of greatest \nrisk. high temperatures will be in the upper 70s and low to mid 80s.\n\nDry conditions return Friday and most of Saturday, then low end (20-\n30 percent) rain and storm chances are possible Sat night into \nMonday. Highs will be in the low to mid 80s.",
